<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="repository-layout" title="Repository layout">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Four things sit under the root. Nine modules build in one reactor, and the other
        three stand outside it.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    repo["Repository root"] --> reactor["Maven reactor"]
    repo --> ide["aether-weaver-ide"]
    repo --> cfg["build-config"]
    repo --> ws["Writerside"]
    reactor --> lib["Seven jars"]
    reactor --> bom["bom"]
    reactor --> t["tests"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="Where a change goes" id="tree">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Path</td><td>Holds</td><td>In the build</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Seven jar modules</td><td>Main sources, unit tests, one pom each</td>
                <td>The reactor</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-bom</code></td><td>A version list and no source</td>
                <td>The reactor</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-tests</code></td>
                <td>Architecture, integration and JMH tests</td>
                <td>The reactor</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A new module</td>
                <td>A pom declaring <code>automatic.module.name</code></td>
                <td>The reactor</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-ide</code></td><td>The IntelliJ plugin and its sample</td>
                <td>Gradle only</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>build-config</code></td><td>Checkstyle, two style contracts, a header</td>
                <td>Read by both</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Writerside</code></td><td>The pages of this site</td>
                <td>Neither build</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A consumer sees nine artefacts, not seven. The BOM is imported by name, and
            the parent resolves behind every jar.
            <a href="artifacts.topic">Artefacts and modules</a> names all nine and what each
            drags in. <a href="building-and-testing.topic">Building and testing</a> is how you
            run any of this.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What refuses a change" id="architecture-tests">
        <p>
            Three tests read the tree as text, never as compiled classes. They sit in
            <code>aether-weaver-tests/src/test/java/.../architecture</code>. That is why they
            catch mistakes the compiler is happy with.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="ProjectStructureTest">
                <p>
                    Scans import lines, file names and pom text. Which module is allowed to
                    reference which is under
                    <a href="artifacts.topic#rules">Rules the build enforces</a>. An
                    <code>import</code> counts as a reference even where only a
                    <code>{@link}</code> needs it, which the
                    <a href="javadoc-policy.topic">JavaDoc policy</a> covers. It also fails on a source file
                    that <code>.gitignore</code> matches, because such a file compiles locally and
                    is absent from the commit.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="JavadocCoverageTest">
                <p>
                    Fails on a declaration that carries no comment, and on a
                    <code>@since</code> tag below a type. What it walks is under
                    <a href="javadoc-policy.topic#scope">what has to carry a comment</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="JavadocStyleTest">
                <p>
                    Refuses wording no comment in this project may use, and reaches further
                    than the reactor compiles. Its scope and its list are under
                    <a href="javadoc-policy.topic#register">wording the style test refuses</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The build that stands apart" id="ide">
        <p>
            <code>aether-weaver-ide</code> sits
            <a href="artifacts.topic#rules">outside the reactor</a>. Working on it costs two
            things a reactor module does not:
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>Gradle resolves the api and the engine from your local Maven repository.
                The processor comes in at test scope. Nothing here compiles until the reactor
                has been installed there.</p></li>
            <li><p>Its Checkstyle task points at <code>build-config/checkstyle.xml</code>, the
                file the reactor uses. One line-length limit governs both.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            One pom does sit further down. <code>aether-weaver-idea/sample</code> is a
            standalone project, <code>com.acme:payments-sample</code>. A task of its own
            builds it against the published api, and the Gradle build reads its version.
            What the plugin does for a user is
            <a href="intellij-plugin.topic">The IntelliJ IDEA plugin</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where the standards live" id="standards">
        <p>
            <code>build-config</code> holds three standards, each with a different reader.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="checkstyle.xml">
                <p>
                    Runs in the <code>validate</code> phase, over main and test sources alike.
                    Most of it is formatting. Three checks are semantic:
                    <code>MissingOverride</code>, <code>EqualsHashCode</code> and
                    <code>StringLiteralEquality</code>. Those reject code that compiles. The
                    <a href="artifacts.topic#rules">module descriptor ban</a> lives here too, and
                    <code>license-header.txt</code> beside it is read by nothing.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="reweave/">
                <p>
                    The JavaDoc house style and its work breakdown. No build step reads either
                    file; <code>mvn verify</code> enforces the outcome instead.
                    <code>JavadocCoverageTest</code> and <code>JavadocStyleTest</code> are two of
                    the four gates. Checkstyle holds the 120-column limit, and a
                    <code>doclint</code> javadoc run resolves every <code>{@link}</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="docsite/">
                <p>
                    The style contract for this site, its page list, and
                    <code>check-docs.py</code>. The pages themselves are under
                    <code>Writerside</code>, and no Maven module touches them.
                    <a href="building-and-testing.topic">Building and testing</a> has the command
                    that checks them.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

